WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:07.770
There are a few incredible records in esports.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:13.420
Take the 2015 Fnatic League of Legends roster,
that went undefeated in their Summer regular season.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.260
Or Smash 4 player ZeRo, who won 56 tournaments
during that game’s first year.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:24.520
Or Ninjas in Pyjamas, who dominated the earliest
days of CS:GO with a 87-map LAN win-streak.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:29.640
But despite all the victories, all the undefeated
records, there are very few teams who've managed to pull off

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.880
the exact opposite.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.180
Only one team was bad enough, unlucky
enough, cosmically unloved enough to pull

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.480
off the longest winless streak in esports history.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.480
One team that totally
overhauled their roster, and kicked their

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.740
best player in the middle of the season.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:50.180
Ladies and gentlemen, pros and scrubs of all
ages, let’s talk about the longest losing streak

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:51.460
in esports history.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:56.300
Let’s talk about the Shanghai Dragons.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.860
The Shanghai Dragons just completed the first
season of the Overwatch League with an unprecedented

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.780
0-40 record.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.159
Yeah you heard that right, 0-40.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.800
We checked, and while plenty of teams have
come close to that record, no one has ever actually achieved it.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.700
At least not in a major esports league.

00:01:13.709 --> 00:01:17.260
There are plenty of League of Legends teams
that have pulled off one win seasons, and

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.500
a good number that went winless, particularly
in the LPL and the LMS.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.340
But a League of Legends split is significantly
shorter than an Overwatch season.

00:01:25.349 --> 00:01:29.820
Notably, there are a few CS:GO teams that
have gone an entire ESL Pro League season

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.720
without winning, but they were all disqualified
before the season ended, so they don’t really count.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.300
But the Shanghai Dragons.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:37.889
Now that is a losing streak.

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:43.880
You have to get really deep into some obscure traditional sports to find any teams that's ever done worse.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:49.979
Across the board, no NFL, NBA, MLB or NHL team
has done worse, with the longest losing streak

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:57.420
there coming from the Philadelphia
76ers, who in 2015 lost 28 games straight across 2 seasons.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.860
"In the opening weeks of OWL, I've had my suspicions growing that for the Shanghai Dragons

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:07.300
the league might actually be a punishment from the ancient Greek underworld."

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:15.060
"The Shanghai Dragons seem condemned to an eternal torture, where victory is perpetually just a little bit out of reach."

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.320
And here’s the weird thing,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.540
by the end of the season.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.969
The Shanghai Dragons roster didn't look that bad on paper.

00:02:19.969 --> 00:02:24.269
They had a strong lineup of Korean players
who were mostly overlooked during the initial signing

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:25.870
blitz ahead of the first season.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:30.400
Geguri, Ado, and Fearless are all solid
players who helped the team improve significantly

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.969
when they were signed partway through the first season.

00:02:31.969 --> 00:02:35.159
But the team’s beginnings were a lot worse.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:40.060
The Shanghai Dragons were the first team to
get a name and brand when the Overwatch League was rolling those out.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.360
A few months later, they revealed
their roster, an all-Chinese team, made up

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.099
of players from various strong Overwatch rosters in China.

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:49.640
But the wheels came off the wagon pretty fast.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:55.940
The team went 0-2 in the pre-season, losing
4-0 to Seoul Dynasty and 3-2 to the Boston Uprising.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:03:31.560
(Casting)

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:36.320
Then in Week 1, they lost to the LA Gladiators and the San Francisco Shock.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:41.020
And then they lost to the Houston Outlaws and the Florida Mayhem, 4-0 both times.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.840
It was around this point people realized that
Shanghai had a problem.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:48.520
And team manager Yang Van released a statement agreeing with that.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:53.160
It was also in that statement that he revealed that the team had a grueling training regimen.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.480
Practicing from 10:30 AM each day until midnight.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:00.400
But Van also said that the team was hungry for
their first victory.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:38.860
(Casting)

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.340
After getting summarily dunked on for
the rest of Stage 1,

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.160
the Dragons returned for Stage 2 with an overhauled roster.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.120
Made up of those aforementioned Korean
players, all of whom were considered some of the best

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.920
in their positions out of players not yet in the Overwatch League.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:35.920
(Casting)

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:37.480
So that didn’t work.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:41.360
After three more weeks of straight losses,
head coach U4 stepped down,

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.080
he was replaced by Kong as interim coach and later
by RUI as the new head coach.

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.300
It mostly did nothing for the team.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.150
They went 0-16 in maps after U4 left.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:56.840
Then, between Stage 2 and 3, the Dragons dropped
Undead, their superstar DPS player.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:01.380
Well, as much of a Star as any 0-20 team can
really have on their roster.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:05.640
Everything related to Undead’s departure
is hearsay, but there were plenty of allegations online

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.320
that he cheated on multiple partners.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:12.030
Either way, even without their star player,
it’s not like things could get much worse

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:13.030
for Shanghai.

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:15.570
In fact, things are starting to look a
little bit better.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:21.379
They picked up their best map record in Stage
3, going 9-0-32, picking up DPS player Daemin

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:22.660
to replace Undead.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:25.360
Things were finally looking up for
the Shanghai Dragons.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:30.420
But whatever light the Shanghai Dragons saw at the
end of their tunnel was quickly covered up by a cave in.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:07:18.860
(Casting)

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:23.180
Shanghai actually ended the fourth and final
stage of the Overwatch League with the worst record

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.060
they’d had since stage 2.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:27.720
That’s right, they actually managed to do worse.

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:50.600
(Casting)

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:57.659
With everything said and done, the Shanghai
Dragons won 21 maps, tied 2, and lost 141.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:01.240
The next-worst team in the league, the Florida Mayhem, won double the amount of maps.

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:05.180
And while they only won seven matches, at
least they made it competitive.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:06.100
Sometimes.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:10.060
And again, it’s not like the Shanghai Dragons
were abject garbage.

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:13.660
A lot of their players were strong, individual players.

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:16.660
But they struggled when it came to playing
as a team.

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:58.460
(Casting)

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:01.980
Part of that might stem from the fact that
Kong has told the Korean players on the team

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.530
not to communicate in Korean during the game.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:09.390
Instead, he’s having the players speak in English
or Chinese, meaning a large chunk of the roster,

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:13.290
arguably the most mechanically solid part,
was having trouble communicating with the

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:14.290
rest of the team.

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:17.140
But no matter the reason, the Shanghai Dragons
did it.

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:21.400
They went a whole season without
a single win and 40 losses.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:25.520
They are officially, the losingest team in
major esports history.

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:26.220
But hey,

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:30.620
There’s no relegation, so at least we get to see
them try it all over again next year.

